.nh
.TH IN-1 1 "September 2026" "in-1" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
in-1 \- instant dev tools for your current shell


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBsource <(curl -sL in-1.cc)\fP [\fB-q\fP | \fB--quiet\fP] \fITOOL\fP\&...
[\fIVAR\fP=\fIVALUE\fP]...

.PP
\fBin-1\fP [\fB-q\fP | \fB--quiet\fP] \fITOOL\fP\&... [\fIVAR\fP=\fIVALUE\fP]...

.PP
\fBin-1\fP \fB-L\fP|\fB--local\fP \fITOOL\fP\&...

.PP
\fBin-1\fP \fB-T\fP|\fB--temp\fP \fITOOL\fP\&...

.PP
\fBin-1\fP \fB--uninstall\fP \fITOOL\fP\&... [\fIPREFIX\fP=\fIDIR\fP]

.PP
\fBin-1\fP \fB--prune\fP [\fITOOL\fP\&...] [\fIPREFIX\fP=\fIDIR\fP]

.PP
\fBin-1\fP \fB--show\fP [\fIPATTERN\fP] [\fIPREFIX\fP=\fIDIR\fP]

.PP
\fBin-1\fP \fB--show\fP \fITOOL\fP[@\fIVERSION\fP]... [\fIPREFIX\fP=\fIDIR\fP]

.PP
\fBin-1\fP \fB--list\fP [\fIPATTERN\fP]

.PP
\fBin-1\fP \fB--version\fP | \fB--help\fP

.PP
\fBin-1\fP \fB--env\fP \fISHELL\fP \fITOOL\fP\&...

.PP
\fBin-1\fP \fB--complete\fP \fISHELL\fP

.PP
\fBin-1\fP \fB--rc\fP

.PP
\fBin-1\fP \fB--update\fP [\fIOPTIONS\fP] [\fITOOL\fP\&...]

.PP
\fBin-1\fP \fB--reset\fP [\fIOPTIONS\fP] [\fITOOL\fP\&...]


.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBin-1\fP installs development tools and languages under a prefix and
puts a single directory, \fI/bin\fP, on \fBPATH\fP for the current
shell session (plus \fBMANPATH\fP and completions).
Nothing else on the system changes.

.PP
Each tool version installs into \fI/share//\fP,
and in-1 writes a small wrapper for every command it provides into
\fI/bin\fP\&.  The wrapper carries the tool's own environment
(CARGO_HOME, ...), so \fBwhich <cmd>\fR is always \fI/bin/\fP
and the shell itself stays clean.
Multiple versions coexist: the primary command is a relative symlink
to its version-specific wrapper, \fI-\fP\&.
Installing another version updates the symlink without changing older
version-specific wrappers.

.PP
The default prefix matches the public installation of in-1.
For example, \fI~/.local/bin/in-1\fP installs tools into \fI~/.local/bin\fP\&.
A fresh bootstrap or source checkout with no installed in-1 defaults to
\fI$TMPDIR/in-1\fP (\fI/tmp/in-1\fP when \fITMPDIR\fP is unset).
The bootstrap clone lives separately in \fI$TMPDIR/in-1/bootstrap\fP\&.
\fB-L\fP or \fB--local\fP selects a persistent prefix;
\fB-T\fP or \fB--temp\fP forces the temporary one.
Installation, \fB--show\fP, \fB--uninstall\fP, \fB--prune\fP, and \fB--reset\fP
share this selection.

.PP
Before an install, in-1 checks whether its own clone is behind its
origin and prints a notice if so.
It automatically updates its makes clone when that clone is behind.
Use \fB--update\fP to update in-1 too, or set \fIIN1_OFFLINE\fP to skip both
update checks.
\fB--reset\fP removes managed tools in the selected prefix and clears state,
while preserving in-1 itself and unrelated files.

.PP
It works in bash, zsh and fish, on Linux and macOS (Intel and ARM),
and needs only \fBgit\fP(1), \fBcurl\fP(1), GNU \fBmake\fP(1) and
\fBbash\fP(1) to be installed.

.PP
Tools are installed by the makes project
(https://github.com/makeplus/makes); any tool with a \fITOOL\fP\&.mk file
there is available.  A tool may also be requested by an alias command
name (e.g. \fBcargo\fP installs rust, \fBbb\fP installs babashka).
Run \fBin-1 --list\fP to see tools and aliases.

.PP
\fBin-1\fP is itself one of the tools: \fBin-1\fP as a \fITOOL\fP (the
one-liner with \fBin-1\fP, say) installs the in-1 command for the
current shell session, complete with its shell function, man page and
completion, and \fBin-1 --local in-1\fP installs it for keeps.
Then \fBsource <(in-1 --rc)\fP (fish: \fBin-1 --rc | source\fP) in a shell
rc file gives every new shell the same.
\fBsource <(curl -sL in-1.cc) --local in-1\fP does both the install and
the setup of the current shell in one go.

.PP
Append \fB@VERSION\fP to a tool or alias to pin its version, for example
\fBin-1 perl@5.42.0\fP or \fBin-1 bb@1.13.220\fP\&.
A pinned version installs alongside others and is reachable as both
\fIperl\fP and its version-specific wrapper.
Install multiple versions with \fBin-1 jolt@0.8.20 jolt@0.8.19\fP\&.
Requests run in argument order; the last listed version becomes active.
Each pin applies only to its own request.

.PP
Any \fIVAR\fP=\fIVALUE\fP argument is passed through to makes as a variable.
\fIPREFIX\fP=\fIDIR\fP is the exception: it sets the install prefix, like the
\fIPREFIX\fP environment variable.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-q\fP, \fB--quiet\fP
  Suppress notes, progress and status output from successful
  operations.
  Failure diagnostics remain visible, and explicit output options such
  as \fB--list\fP, \fB--show\fP, \fB--version\fP and \fB--help\fP still print output.
  Quiet takes precedence over \fIIN1_VERBOSE\fP\&.

.PP
\fB-L\fP, \fB--local\fP
  Select the existing persistent in-1 prefix, otherwise \fI~/.local\fP
  (\fI/usr/local\fP when root).
  An explicit \fIPREFIX\fP overrides this default.
  These installs persist across shell sessions and need no shell
  setup, since the wrappers carry the tools' environment.
  Through the one-liner (\fBsource <(curl -sL in-1.cc) --local\fP \fITOOL\fP)
  the current shell also forgets any stale command paths, and a
  \fB--local in-1\fP sources the installed in-1's \fI\&.rc\fP right away.

.PP
\fB-T\fP, \fB--temp\fP
  Always select \fI$TMPDIR/in-1\fP (default \fI/tmp/in-1\fP), with command wrappers
  directly in its \fIbin\fP directory and state under \fIshare/in-1/local\fP\&.
  Ignore inherited \fIPREFIX\fP and \fIIN1_ROOT\fP\&.
  Reject a \fIPREFIX\fP=\fIDIR\fP argument or \fB--local\fP in the same invocation.
  For example, \fBin-1 --temp --show\fP lists temporary installations.
  An old conflicting temporary checkout is left untouched; move it aside
  before retrying with the new layout.

.PP
\fB--uninstall\fP \fITOOL\fP\&...
  Remove the installs of the given tools from \fIPREFIX\fP:
  \fIPREFIX/share/\fP with every version in it, and every wrapper
  in \fIPREFIX/bin\fP that in-1 wrote for it.
  Files in \fIPREFIX/bin\fP that in-1 did not write stay.
  Append \fB@VERSION\fP to remove only a specific version, for example
  \fBin-1 --uninstall jolt@0.8.20\fP\&.
  Multiple selectors work too:
  \fBin-1 --uninstall jolt@0.8.20 jolt@0.8.19\fP\&.
  Other versions and their wrappers remain installed.
  Removing the active version also removes its current command and shell
  support; another version is not automatically activated.
  Reinstall a remaining version to select it again.
  Use the same prefix as installation, whether called directly or through
  the shell function.
  The curl one-liner rejects \fB--uninstall\fP; install the \fBin-1\fP
  command into the session first.
  Use \fB--temp\fP or an explicit \fIPREFIX\fP to select another installation.
  Aliases work here too (\fBin-1 --uninstall bb\fP removes babashka) and
  \fBin-1 --uninstall in-1\fP removes the command itself.
  \fB--reset\fP removes other managed tools but preserves in-1 itself.

.PP
\fB--prune\fP [\fITOOL\fP\&...]
  Remove other managed versions of each named tool, keeping the currently
  selected version even after a downgrade or prerelease installation.
  With no tools, prune all installed tools, including in-1 itself.
  Aliases are accepted and duplicate requests are combined.
  Remove wrappers targeting discarded versions along with their install trees.
  Preserve the retained version, its shell support, unrelated files, and
  in-1 state, logs and download caches.
  Use active-version links or current primary-command wrappers to identify
  the retained version.
  If neither identifies one version, use the newest successful-install marker.
  Ambiguous tools remain unchanged and produce an error.
  Missing explicitly requested tools also produce errors; other tools continue.
  Empty prefixes and tools already down to one version succeed.
  Prefix selection follows installation, including \fB--local\fP, \fB--temp\fP,
  and \fIPREFIX\fP=\fIDIR\fP\&.
  Pruning never initializes Makes, installs or updates anything, and ignores
  \fIIN1_UPDATE\fP\&.
  Reject make variables and combinations with \fB--uninstall\fP, \fB--reset\fP,
  \fB--update\fP, \fB--list\fP, or \fB--show\fP\&.
  The curl one-liner rejects \fB--prune\fP; use the installed command.
  Through \fB--env\fP, pruning emits no shell code.
  \fB--quiet\fP suppresses successful summaries; errors remain visible.

.PP
\fB--list\fP [\fIPATTERN\fP]
  List all available tool names and command aliases.
  Aliases appear as \fBbb (babashka)\fP; direct tool names are unannotated.
  Tab completion displays alias targets when there are multiple matches,
  but inserts only the command name.
  The optional pattern filters displayed lines using case-sensitive
  \fBgrep -E\fP syntax, including alias labels.
  Omit it to list everything; no matches succeed with no output.
  Invalid patterns fail.
  Use \fB--\fP before a pattern starting with a dash.

.PP
\fB--show\fP [\fITOOL\fP[@\fIVERSION\fP]...] | [\fIPATTERN\fP]
  Show installed tools in aligned columns: canonical tool name, version,
  disk usage, and absolute installation directory.
  Sort tool names alphabetically and their versions from most recently
  installed to oldest, using successful-install marker timestamps.
  Legacy command installs use the newest managed wrapper timestamp instead.
  Equal timestamps retain version-name order.
  Disk usage matches \fBdu -sh\fP run separately on each installation.
  Include completed libraries and version directories referenced by an
  in-1 wrapper; omit unrelated directories and unreferenced leftovers.
  On a terminal, the highest installed version is bold, and other versions
  of the selected tool are grey.
  The selected version follows the same links and markers used by \fB--prune\fP\&.
  Determine newest and selected versions before filtering the rows.
  Piped output is plain; \fINO_COLOR\fP or \fITERM=dumb\fP disables styling.
  Accept multiple tool names or aliases, optionally qualified with \fB@VERSION\fP\&.
  For example, \fBin-1 --show bb jolt\fP lists both tools, and
  \fBin-1 --show bb@1.13.226 jolt@0.8.20\fP lists those exact versions.
  Selectors match exact tool names and versions; overlapping selectors show
  each installation only once.
  A single pattern can also filter whole rows using case-sensitive
  \fBgrep -E\fP syntax, so names, versions, sizes and paths can all be matched.
  Installed tool names and known aliases take precedence over patterns.
  Regex patterns cannot be combined with tool selectors.
  Omit arguments to show everything, or use \fB--\fP before a pattern starting
  with a dash.
  No matches produce no output and succeed; an invalid pattern fails.
  Prefix selection follows installation and \fB--uninstall\fP\&.
  \fIPREFIX\fP=\fIDIR\fP overrides the environment and default, except with \fB--temp\fP\&.
  This operation does not install or update anything, ignores \fIIN1_UPDATE\fP,
  and rejects \fB--update\fP and \fB--reset\fP\&.

.PP
\fB--env\fP \fISHELL\fP
  Install the tools, then print environment setup code for \fISHELL\fP
  (\fBbash\fP, \fBzsh\fP or \fBfish\fP) on stdout.
  This is the plumbing used by the sourced one-liner and by the
  \fBin-1\fP shell function; you can use it directly:
  \fBeval "$(in-1 --env bash rust)"\fP

.PP
\fB--complete\fP \fISHELL\fP
  Print tab completion code for the \fBin-1\fP command itself.

.PP
\fB--rc\fP
  Print the line that sets up the shell: \fBsource\fP followed by the
  path of this in-1's own \fI\&.rc\fP\&.
  Put \fBsource <(in-1 --rc)\fP (bash, zsh) or \fBin-1 --rc | source\fP
  (fish) in your shell rc file to get the \fBin-1\fP shell function, man
  page and tab completion in every shell.
  It also restores the man pages and completions provided by installed
  tools, without running those tools during shell startup.
  Since it goes through the \fBin-1\fP on \fIPATH\fP, the line keeps working
  after \fBin-1 --local in-1\fP installs a newer version.

.PP
\fB--update\fP
  Update the in-1 clone and the makes clone under \fIIN1_ROOT\fP before
  doing anything else.
  When \fIIN1_ROOT\fP is a persistent install's state directory, update
  the clone containing the running command instead of that directory.
  Alone, that is all it does; with tools or other options it then
  continues with them, so \fBin-1 --update rust\fP gets the newest in-1 and
  makes and then installs rust.
  The in-1 clone moves to the latest default branch (or to
  \fIIN1_VERSION\fP if set) and the one-liner leaves it there from then
  on.

.PP
\fB--reset\fP
  Remove wrapper-backed tool versions and their wrappers from the selected
  prefix, and clear \fImakes/\fP, \fIlog/\fP and \fIcache/\fP under \fIIN1_ROOT\fP\&.
  Preserve in-1's own installation, unrelated files, old nested installs,
  and a cache placed elsewhere with \fIIN1_CACHE\fP\&.
  The whole prefix is never removed.
  Alone, that is all it does; with tools or other options it then
  continues with them, so \fBin-1 --reset rust\fP installs rust from
  scratch and \fBin-1 --reset --update\fP resets and then updates.

.PP
\fB--version\fP
  Print the in-1 version.

.PP
\fB--help\fP
  Print usage help.


.SH ENVIRONMENT
\fBIN1_ROOT\fP
  State directory for Makes, logs and the download cache.
  Default: \fIPREFIX/share/in-1/local\fP, independent of tool versions.
  It does not determine where command wrappers are installed.
  The one-liner and \fB--temp\fP ignore an inherited value.
  A sourced development checkout may use its own directory for state.
  Sourcing \fI\&.rc\fP exports it.

.PP
\fBIN1_CACHE\fP
  Download cache directory.
  Default: \fI$IN1_ROOT/cache\fP\&.

.PP
\fBPREFIX\fP
  Install prefix.
  Default: the public prefix of an installed in-1, otherwise \fI$TMPDIR/in-1\fP\&.
  \fB--local\fP falls back to \fI~/.local\fP or \fI/usr/local\fP when root.
  A relative path is anchored to the current directory.
  A \fIPREFIX\fP=\fIDIR\fP argument takes precedence over the environment.
  A trailing \fI/bin\fP is stripped with a warning.
  \fB--temp\fP ignores the environment and rejects the argument form.

.PP
\fBIN1_VERSION\fP
  The in-1 git ref to use.

.PP
\fBIN1_REPO\fP
  The in-1 repo URL to clone.
  Default: \fIhttps://github.com/in-1-cc/in-1\fP\&.

.PP
\fBIN1_MAKES_REPO\fP
  The makes repo URL to clone.
  Default: \fIhttps://github.com/makeplus/makes\fP\&.

.PP
\fBIN1_UPDATE\fP
  Set to 1 for the same effect as \fB--update\fP\&.

.PP
\fBIN1_OFFLINE\fP
  Set to 1 to skip the update checks before installs, including the
  automatic makes update.

.PP
\fBIN1_VERBOSE\fP
  Set to 1 to stream the full install output instead of the quiet
  per-tool progress lines.
  \fB-q\fP and \fB--quiet\fP take precedence when either is used.

.PP
\fBIN1_TOOLS\fP
  Set by in-1 in the shell; lists the active tools.


.SH EXAMPLES
Try Rust and Node temporarily, even with a persistent in-1:

.EX
source <(curl -sL in-1.cc) --temp rust node
.EE

.PP
Same, from fish:

.EX
curl -sL in-1.cc | source - --temp rust node
.EE

.PP
Pin a version (both \fBgo\fR and \fBgo-1.23.4\fR end up on PATH):

.EX
source <(curl -sL in-1.cc) go@1.23.4
.EE

.PP
Install a tool by an alias command name:

.EX
source <(curl -sL in-1.cc) cargo
.EE

.PP
Get the in-1 command itself in the current shell:

.EX
source <(curl -sL in-1.cc) in-1
.EE

.PP
Install the in-1 command permanently, either way:

.EX
in-1 --local in-1
echo 'source <(in-1 --rc)' >> ~/.bashrc

git clone https://github.com/in-1-cc/in-1 ~/.in-1
echo 'source ~/.in-1/.rc' >> ~/.bashrc
.EE

.PP
Keep jq around for good:

.EX
in-1 --local jq
.EE

.PP
And remove it again:

.EX
in-1 --uninstall jq
.EE

.PP
Update in-1 and makes, then install the newest node:

.EX
in-1 --update node
.EE

.PP
Remove other managed tools and start over with node, keeping in-1:

.EX
in-1 --reset node
.EE


